@article{TZIRITIS2026136130,
title = {Nitrate persistence and inland salinisation in an intensively irrigated Mediterranean river basin district: Diagnostic evidence from WFD monitoring data (Thessaly, central Greece)},
journal = {Journal of Hydrology},
volume = {679},
pages = {136130},
year = {2026},
issn = {0022-1694},
doi = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136130},
url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022169426012278},
author = {Evangelos Tziritis and Dimitra Siakka and Vasilios Pisinaras},
keywords = {Groundwater quality, Nitrate contamination, Inland salinisation, Water framework directive, Bootstrap exceedance, Shannon entropy, Mediterranean aquifer},
abstract = {Intensive irrigation in semi-arid Mediterranean basins generates two co-occurring but mechanistically distinct groundwater stressors, nitrate contamination from fertiliser leaching and inland salinisation from ionic enrichment processes, that standard regulatory assessments fail to capture as a linked system. This study develops a probabilistic diagnostic framework and applies it to five years of Water Framework Directive (WFD) compliance monitoring data (2019–2023, 98 stations, 964 samples) from the Thessaly River Basin District, central Greece, one of Europe’s most groundwater-dependent agricultural systems. Multi-year median nitrate exceeded the 50 mg/L WFD threshold at 15.3% of stations and the 25 mg/L Nitrates Directive alert level at 28.6%; annual basin-wide medians remained within a narrow range (12.4–17.9 mg/L), consistent with quasi-steady-state release from legacy vadose zone stores. A bootstrap exceedance analysis (10,000 iterations) identified six stations where the 95% confidence interval for P(NO3− > 50 mg/L) exceeded 0.50, three of which were located within officially compliant water bodies. Salinisation was spatially restricted, chloride-dominated (96.5% of high-EC records), and nearly completely decoupled from the nitrate footprint (7.1% co-exceedance). Na/Cl molar ratios and Gibbs diagram positioning indicate that evapoconcentrative and evaporitic processes are dominant salinisation mechanisms; however, ambiguous ionic signatures at a small number of stations preclude the definitive exclusion of relict or deep saline sources without isotopic confirmation. A Shannon entropy-weighted Compound Vulnerability Index and a classification concordance analysis (Cohen’s κ = 0.273) demonstrate that WFD spatial aggregation at the water body level systematically underestimates the active contamination footprint. The framework is reproducible from standard WISE data and can be directly transferred to any river basin district reporting WFD.}
}
@article{LI2026136173,
title = {InSAR-driven characterization of “ungauged” aquifer systems: Insights from the Gediz River Basin, Türkiye},
journal = {Journal of Hydrology},
volume = {679},
pages = {136173},
year = {2026},
issn = {0022-1694},
doi = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136173},
url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022169426012709},
author = {Yueting Li and Alper Elçi and Claudia Meisina and Elif Aysu Batkan and Ali Hakan Ören and Pietro Teatini and Claudia Zoccarato},
keywords = {Aquifer characterization, Groundwater exploitation, Land subsidence, InSAR, Hydro-geomechanical modeling},
abstract = {Accurate characterization of key hydro-geomechanical properties, specifically hydraulic conductivity K and specific storage Ss, is essential for predicting the responses of aquifer systems to natural and anthropogenic stressors and ensuring sustainable groundwater management. However, such characterization remains particularly challenging in “ungauged” basins, i.e., where piezometric records, pumping test data, and information about production wells are sparse or unavailable. We propose an iterative framework based on coupling a three-dimensional (3D) groundwater flow (GW) model with a 3D geomechanical (GM) model, using a nested inversion scheme. The workflow begins with calibrating the GW model against piezometric records to acquire an initial estimation of heterogeneous K distribution given a homogeneous Ss. The resulting hydraulic head changes are then fused with Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar (InSAR) measurements to estimate point-wise Ss values that are subsequently interpolated across the basin scale. This Ss field is validated using the GM model and fed back into the GW model for a next iteration of K calibration. This iterative cycle continues until the estimated parameters stabilize and further updates provide limited improvements in model performance. We apply this approach to the Gediz River Basin (GRB) in western Türkiye, a region experiencing severe land subsidence from groundwater over-exploitation for irrigation. Our results reveal significant heterogeneity in aquifer properties, and the inferred K and Ss distributions allow for the reproduction of both hydraulic head evolution and land subsidence patterns, with a consistent modeling set-up.}
}
@article{WANG2026136196,
title = {Quantifying hillslope-to-river phosphorus contributions to water quality through a cascading framework of hydrological connectivity},
journal = {Journal of Hydrology},
volume = {679},
pages = {136196},
year = {2026},
issn = {0022-1694},
doi = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.136196},
url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S002216942601293X},
author = {Yanan Wang and Guishan Yang and Weixin Ou and Hengpeng Li},
keywords = {Landscape graphs, Cascading effect, Nutrient transport, Watershed management},
abstract = {Quantifying nutrient transport in heterogeneous catchments remains challenging due to the oversimplification of lateral terrestrial interactions in semi-distributed models. this study developed a cascading-effect-enhanced Soil and Water Assessment Tool (SWAT) framework (SWAT-CE) for total phosphorus (TP) simulation. By integrating a directed acyclic graph with a precipitation-driven Spatio-temporal Connectivity-Decay Function, this framework explicitly simulates hillslope nutrient routing. The results indicate that incorporating the cascading mechanism better captures the source–pathway–sink dynamics of phosphorus transport. Spatially, cascading correction shifted TP loads from fragmented upslope source patches toward more continuous accumulation zones in the mid- and downstream areas. After cascading correction, rice paddies emerged as important collection and relay nodes, contributing 12.76% and 24.09% of TP in August and September, respectively. By contrast, low-sand soils and steep-slope units (>15°) mainly acted as source areas. Sankey analysis identified a stable seasonal cascading architecture: during the flood season, tea plantations were the dominant sources, with the “Tea plantation–High sand–Low slope” class contributing 11.01% of total flux, whereas in the non-flood season orchards became dominant (26.66%). Forests functioned as persistent sink nodes, receiving 16.36% and 17.96% of flux in the flood and non-flood seasons, respectively. Overall, SWAT-CE provides a more realistic basis for TP hotspot identification and targeted watershed phosphorus management. This study provides a robust, transferable methodology for precision management, shifting the focus from uniform source control to topologically-informed landscape optimization.}
}
